        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.46,
            0.146,
            0.591,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": "head : torso"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.46,
            0.175,
            0.597,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": "\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ : \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.46,
            0.201,
            0.591,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": "torso : arms"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.46,
            0.255,
            0.579,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "torso : legs"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.747,
            0.151,
            0.86,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.764,
            0.245,
            0.851,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.364,
            0.848,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "Now, draw a figure with head, torso, arms, and legs with equivalent ratios as above."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.412,
            0.812,
            0.452
          ],
          "content": "？ Does the drawing look more realistic if the ratios are proportional? Why? Why not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.831,
            0.397,
            0.916,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.463,
            0.837,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "**Note to the Teacher:** In all these activities, encourage students to reason why their drawings are proportional."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.538,
            0.566,
            0.561
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Trairasika—The Rule of Three"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.571,
            0.848,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 8:** For the mid-day meal in a school with 120 students, the cook usually makes 15 kg of rice. On a rainy day, only 80 students came to school. How many kilograms of rice should the cook make so that the food is not wasted?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.65,
            0.847,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": "The ratio of the number of students to the amount of rice needs to be proportional."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.406,
            0.687,
            0.593,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": "\\S 0 , 1 2 0 : 1 5 : : 8 0 : ?",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.714,
            0.598,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": "What is the factor of change in the first term?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.738,
            0.598,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": "We can find that by dividing the terms ${ \\frac { 8 0 } { 1 2 0 } } = { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.766,
            0.65,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "The number of students is reduced by a factor of $\\frac { 2 } { 3 }$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.793,
            0.763,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": "On multiplying the weight of rice by the same factor, we get,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.457,
            0.816,
            0.565,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "1 5 \\times { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } } = 1 0 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.844,
            0.641,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": "So, the cook should make 10 kg of rice on that day."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.867,
            0.849,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": "The situation above is a typical example of a problem where we need to use proportional reasoning to find a solution. Four quantities are"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.872,
